"""Adult life essentials, unit 2: the people in my life."""
from spec import L, U

B = U('The people in my life', 'Morality', 'How do I deal honestly and lovingly with the people closest to me?', [
L('Anger, grudges and the long road of forgiveness',
  'Anger is not itself a sin, but it is a sign that something matters, and what we do with it decides everything. Forgiveness is not a feeling we can produce on demand. It is a decision, a prayer and a slow work that Jesus puts at the centre of the Our Father, and it is closer to healing than to excusing.',
  '2302-2306,2838-2845,1933,1422-1424',
  'Matthew 5:21-26;Matthew 6:12-15;Matthew 18:21-35;Ephesians 4:26-27',
  'forgiveness mercy peace sin',
  'Whom have you not forgiven, and what has it cost you to keep that wound?',
  'Write a two-column page. On the left, what was done to you and how it hurt, in plain words. On the right, three small steps toward forgiveness that are within your power this month (pray for them by name, stop retelling it, a letter you do not send). Keep the page private.',
  'pater',
  id='YA-L4',
  hook='Ask: “What is the longest grudge you have held?” Do not answer aloud. Then read Matthew 18:21–22 and ask: “Why would Peter think seven times is generous?”',
  teach=[
    'Anger first. The Catechism does not condemn the feeling: “Anger is a desire for revenge,” and it is sinful only when it wills to harm, or when it is deliberate and disordered (CCC 2302–2303). Jesus’ own anger at the temple (Mark 11:15–17) and Paul’s “be angry but do not sin” (Ephesians 4:26) show that anger can be right, if it is about justice and short-lived. The gravest danger is when it becomes hatred, a settled will to harm (CCC 2302–2303).',
    'Forgiveness is the single petition of the Our Father that Jesus explains. Right after the prayer he adds: “If you forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father also will forgive you” (Matthew 6:14–15). The Catechism puts it plainly: “It is not in our power not to feel or to forget an offense; but the heart that offers itself to the Holy Spirit turns injury into compassion and purifies the memory in transforming the hurt into intercession” (CCC 2843).',
    'So forgiveness is not a feeling, and it is not a verdict that nothing happened. It does not require you to trust, to reconcile, or to stay in danger. Justice can still be pursued, boundaries kept, and truth told (CCC 2305, 1933). What forgiveness refuses is the will to revenge and the daily rehearsal of the wound. It begins with prayer: bless those who curse you (Luke 6:28).',
    'The Church gives a path. The sacrament of Penance is where we receive forgiveness so that we can give it (CCC 1422–1424, 2842). And the Catechism’s deep claim is that unforgiveness shuts the door we ourselves need: “This ‘as’ is not unique in Jesus’ teaching” (CCC 2842). We are not asked to forgive in order to earn mercy, but because mercy received and mercy given are one movement.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is the difference between forgiving, excusing, forgetting, and trusting again?',
    'Is there anger in your life that is trying to tell you something true? What?',
    'What would it mean to pray for someone you have not forgiven, by name, for thirty days?',
    'Does forgiving require you to stay in an unsafe situation? What does the Church say?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'According to the Catechism, is anger always sinful?|Yes, always|No, it becomes sinful when it wills harm or is disordered|Only in the young|Only when expressed aloud|1|CCC 2302–2303.',
    'What does CCC 2843 say forgiveness does?|It forces us to forget|It turns injury into compassion and the hurt into intercession|It makes the wrongdoer innocent|It removes the need for justice|1|CCC 2843.',
    'Does forgiving someone require you to trust them again or stay in danger?|Yes, always|No: forgiveness and trust are different, and safety and justice remain|Only if they apologize|Only if they are family|1|CCC 2305 and 1933: peace and justice are not opposed to forgiveness.',
  ],
  life='Choose one person you find hard to forgive. For thirty days, pray for them by name once a day: “Lord, bless N. and give them what they truly need.” Do not retell the story to others in that time. If it involves real harm, also seek counsel from a priest or counselor.',
  saint='Maria Goretti: an eleven-year-old Italian girl who forgave her murderer on her deathbed. He was later converted, and he attended her canonization.',
  misc=[
    '“Forgiveness means saying it did not matter.”',
    '“If I am still angry, I have not really forgiven.”',
    '“Forgiving means going back to how things were.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:confession', 'room:aug-sermon-mount'],
  mem='“Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.” (The Our Father)',
  assess='Ask each person to explain what forgiveness is and is not, using CCC 2843 and one example of a safe boundary.',
  script=dict(
    open='Write on a board: “Whom have you not forgiven?” Do not discuss. Say, “Just look at it. Everyone has a name that comes. Hold that name; we will not say it aloud.” Wait. Then say, “Now let’s see what Jesus says.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Anger first. It is not a sin by itself. The Catechism, 2302, says anger is a desire for revenge. It becomes a sin when we will to hurt, or when it is out of order. Jesus got angry in the temple. Paul says, ‘Be angry but do not sin.’ So anger is a signal. The danger is when it hardens into hatred.”',
      'Say: “Now the Our Father. Out of seven petitions, only one gets a comment from Jesus. ‘If you forgive others their trespasses, your Father will forgive you.’ So the one petition he explains is forgiveness. Why? Because we cannot receive mercy while holding a fist.”',
      'Say: “But what is forgiveness? The Catechism, 2843, says: ‘It is not in our power not to feel or to forget an offense. But the heart that offers itself to the Holy Spirit turns injury into compassion and purifies the memory by turning the hurt into intercession.’ So forgiveness is not a feeling. It is a decision, and a prayer. It does not mean trust. It does not mean going back. It does not cancel justice.”',
      'Say: “The path is small. Pray for the person by name. Stop retelling the story. Receive forgiveness yourself in Confession. And if it is a deep wound, ask for help; forgiveness can take years. The Church does not ask you to feel it first. It asks you to start.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What do people usually confuse forgiveness with?',
      'What small step toward forgiveness is in your power this week?',
      'How would your life be different if you were free of that grudge?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give out a sheet with two columns. On the left write the hurt in plain words. On the right write three small steps within your power.',
      'In pairs, say only: “One step I will take this week is ____.”',
      'Write the name of the person on a slip and place it in the middle with a candle. Pray the Our Father slowly.',
    ],
    close='Say: “Forgiveness is not finished in a night. But it begins with a name and a prayer.” Make the Sign of the Cross.'),
  tryit=['Write the hurt in plain words.', 'Write three small steps you can take.', 'Pray the Our Father slowly for the one name.'],
  saintStory='Maria Goretti was born in Italy in 1890, the child of poor farmers. When her father died of malaria, the family worked a farm shared with another family, the Serenellis. Maria, at eleven, kept house and cared for her younger brothers and sisters while her mother worked the fields.\nAlessandro Serenelli, eighteen, began to pursue her. She refused him, saying that it was a sin and that God did not want it. On July 5, 1902, he attacked her. She fought him and told him, “No, it is a sin! God does not want it!” He stabbed her fourteen times. She lived another day. In the hospital she forgave him and said she wanted him with her in heaven.\nAlessandro was sentenced to thirty years. For years he was bitter. Then Maria appeared to him in a dream, giving him white lilies. He changed, and when he left prison he asked forgiveness from her mother, who gave it. Maria was canonized in 1950; her mother and Alessandro, then an old man, stood in the crowd.',
),

L('Truth in small things: speech, gossip and the people we talk about',
  'Most of us will never commit perjury, but we shape other people’s reputations every day with a sentence. The eighth commandment is about the quiet damage of exaggerating, flattering, spreading what is true but not ours to tell, and judging motives. It is also about courage: the Church treats truthful speech as a form of love and a form of justice.',
  '2464-2470,2475-2487,2488-2492,2507,1807',
  'Ephesians 4:25-32;James 3:1-12;Matthew 7:1-5;Proverbs 18:21',
  'justice virtue charity',
  'What did you say about someone this week that you would not have said to their face?',
  'For two days, keep a tally of every time you talk about someone who is not present. Mark each as helpful, neutral, or unkind. At the end, choose one kind of talk you will drop and one kind of good you will do in its place (praise to their face, a handwritten note).',
  'morning-offering',
  id='YA-L5',
  hook='Pass a sheet of paper around the circle. Each person writes one sentence about an imaginary person “Pat.” At the end read all sentences aloud as if they were one story. Ask: “What happened to Pat? How easily did a story grow?”',
  teach=[
    'The eighth commandment is more than “do not lie.” It protects the right of every person to truth and to a good name (CCC 2464–2470). It names four offenses against reputation: rash judgment (assuming a fault without foundation), detraction (revealing someone’s faults to people who have no right to know them), calumny (saying false things to harm), and flattery (praise that serves our own advantage; CCC 2477–2480). Note that detraction can be true.',
    'The Catechism’s rule of charity is surprisingly demanding: “To avoid rash judgment, everyone should be careful to interpret as far as possible his neighbor’s thoughts, words, and deeds in a favorable way” (CCC 2478). And if you cannot, ask them: “Let him ask how the other understands it” (CCC 2478). Most gossip would collapse if we just did this.',
    'Lying is “to speak or act against the truth in order to lead into error someone who has the right to know the truth” (CCC 2483). It is a wound to trust, and trust is the substance of any community (CCC 2486). But truth is not a weapon; there is also the duty to keep secrets, to respect privacy, and to be silent when discretion or professional secrecy requires it (CCC 2488–2492).',
    'Courage belongs here too. Truth also means speaking up for those unjustly slandered and refusing to join in cruel laughter. Martyrdom, the Catechism notes, is the supreme witness to truth (CCC 2473). Most of us will be asked for smaller witnesses: to say, “That is not what I know,” to refuse a lie at work, to apologise first.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Which of the four offenses (rash judgment, detraction, calumny, flattery) do you most easily slip into?',
    'When is it right to tell something unflattering about a person, and to whom?',
    'How can you speak truth in love at work or in your family?',
    'Is silence ever a form of lying?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'Which of these is detraction?|Telling a false story to hurt someone|Telling true faults to people who have no right to know them|Praising someone to get something|Assuming someone is guilty without evidence|1|CCC 2477: detraction reveals faults without good reason.',
    'What does CCC 2478 say we should do to avoid rash judgment?|Assume the worst to be safe|Interpret a neighbor’s words and deeds in a favorable way, or ask them|Say nothing ever|Rely on first impressions|1|CCC 2478.',
    'What is a lie, according to CCC 2483?|Any false statement|To speak or act against the truth to lead into error someone who has a right to know the truth|Any joke|Keeping a secret|1|CCC 2483.',
  ],
  life='Take the “fast from talking about people who are not here” for one week. If you slip, simply say, “I should not have said that.” Each day, say one specific, true, good thing about someone to their face or in a note.',
  saint='Thomas More: a lawyer and statesman beheaded in 1535 for refusing to take an oath he believed false. He said, “I die the king’s good servant, but God’s first.”',
  misc=[
    '“If it is true, it is not gossip.”',
    '“A white lie is always harmless.”',
    '“Being honest means saying everything I think.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:aug-moral', 'room:desales-devout-life'],
  mem='“Let no evil talk come out of your mouths, but only such as is good for edifying.” (Ephesians 4:29)',
  assess='Ask each person to name the four offenses against reputation, give one example of each from ordinary life, and say what they will do differently.',
  script=dict(
    open='Whisper a short sentence to the person on your left: “Sam was seen near the office late on Friday.” Ask each person to whisper it on. At the end, read what the last person heard. Ask, “What happened to the sentence?”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The eighth commandment, ‘You shall not bear false witness,’ starts in a courtroom but ends in the kitchen. Catechism 2464 says it forbids misrepresenting the truth in our relations with others. The deeper idea: every person has a right to truth and to a good name. When we shape what others think of someone, we hold something that belongs to them.”',
      'Say: “The Catechism names four sins. Rash judgment: assuming someone’s fault without good reason. Detraction: telling someone’s faults, even true ones, to people who have no need to know them. Calumny: telling lies to harm. Flattery: praising to get something. Notice that detraction can be completely accurate. A true story told for no good reason can still hurt.”',
      'Say: “Here is the rule of charity from 2478: interpret your neighbour’s words and actions in a favourable way as far as possible. And if you cannot, ask them. Most gossip dies if you just do that. Next time someone tells you something about a person, ask: ‘Have you talked with them?’”',
      'Say: “Truth is also courage. A witness is someone who says what is so even when it costs. Most of us will not be martyrs, but we will be asked to say, ‘That is not true,’ or ‘I was wrong,’ at work, in a family, in a group chat. Truth is a form of love.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Think of the last time someone said something about you that was not quite true. How did it feel?',
      'Which of the four sins do you find easiest? What triggers it?',
      'What is a specific, true, good thing you could say about someone this week?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Pass out a card. On one side write: “One kind of talk I will drop.” On the other: “One good thing I will say to someone’s face.”',
      'In pairs, tell each other only the second side and who it is for.',
      'Write the first sentence of the note or message you will send, and send it before you leave.',
    ],
    close='Pray the morning offering aloud together, and say: “Words are small, and they build or burn. Choose them.”'),
  tryit=['Count the people you talk about for two days.', 'Choose one kind of talk to drop.', 'Say one true good thing to someone’s face.'],
  saintStory='Thomas More was born in London in 1478. He became a lawyer, a scholar, and a friend of Erasmus, and wrote Utopia. He was a loving husband and father, who made sure his daughters were educated at a time when that was unusual. In 1529 King Henry VIII made him Lord Chancellor, the highest office in the realm after the king.\nBut Henry wanted his marriage to Catherine annulled so that he could marry Anne Boleyn, and when the Pope refused, Henry declared himself head of the Church in England. More resigned in 1532. He kept silent, but in 1534 he was asked to swear an oath that accepted the king as supreme head of the Church, and he refused. He was imprisoned in the Tower of London for fifteen months. His family begged him to give in. He answered that he would not lie before God.\nHe was beheaded on July 6, 1535. On the scaffold he said he was dying “the king’s good servant, but God’s first.” He was canonized in 1935 and is the patron saint of lawyers and statesmen.',
),

L('Friendship, loneliness and the Church as family',
  'Loneliness is one of the most widespread sufferings of our time, and the Church’s teaching on the communion of saints, charity, friendship and the domestic Church speaks to it directly. We are made for communion; the faith offers real, practical places to belong, including for people who are single, widowed, or far from family.',
  '946-962,1822-1829,2347,1655-1658,1878-1885,2212',
  'John 15:9-17;Acts 2:42-47;Ecclesiastes 4:9-12;Romans 12:9-16',
  'communion-of-saints charity church solidarity',
  'Who would notice within a week if you disappeared, and who would you notice?',
  'Name three people you could be a friend to in the next month: one you already love, one you have drifted from, one you barely know. Write one specific act for each (a meal, a call, an invitation) and put the first on your calendar.',
  'before-meals',
  id='YA-L6',
  hook='Ask: “When did you last eat a meal with another person with no screens?” Silence. Then read John 15:15, “I have called you friends,” and ask: “Why would Jesus say that to people who deserted him?”',
  teach=[
    '“God did not make us to be alone.” The Catechism says it is part of the human person to be social: “Man is a social being” (CCC 1878–1879), made in the image of a God who is himself a communion of persons (CCC 221, 2205). Loneliness is therefore not a personal failure but a signal of a real need. The faith responds to it with a body, not an idea.',
    'The communion of saints (CCC 946–962) is the belief that all the baptized, living and dead, are one family sharing spiritual goods: prayer, sacrifice, merits. “Since all the faithful form one body, the good of each is communicated to the others” (CCC 947). In practice that means we are never praying alone, and there are saints in heaven who are real friends, with the same Lord, to whom we can talk.',
    'Charity is the form of every friendship that lasts. It is “the theological virtue by which we love God above all things for his own sake, and our neighbor as ourselves” (CCC 1822). Its fruits include joy, peace, and mercy (CCC 1829). The Church’s teaching on chastity adds: “Chastity is expressed notably in friendship with one’s neighbor. Whether it develops between persons of the same or opposite sex, friendship represents a great good for all” (CCC 2347).',
    'For people who live alone, the Catechism gives a piece of teaching most never hear: “We must also remember the great number of persons who remain single on account of the particular conditions in which they must live... These persons are especially close to the heart of Jesus,” and are welcomed into the “domestic church” as part of the great family of the Church (CCC 1658). Belonging is made, by showing up regularly, by serving, by letting people in.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Where do you feel most alone, and what does it make you want?',
    'What stops you from reaching out first?',
    'How can a parish or small group become a place of real friendship, not just a service?',
    'Who in your life or parish is easy to overlook, and what could you do?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the communion of saints mean?|Only the canonized saints|All the faithful, living and dead, are one family sharing spiritual goods|A group of priests|A prayer book|1|CCC 946–953.',
    'What does CCC 2347 say about friendship?|It is a distraction|Chastity is expressed in friendship, which is a great good for all|It is only for family|Friendship is a private matter|1|CCC 2347.',
    'What does CCC 1658 say about people who live alone?|They are second-class|They are especially close to the heart of Jesus and welcomed into the family of the Church|They must marry|They have no vocation|1|CCC 1658.',
  ],
  life='Choose one regular, recurring act of presence for the next month: a weekly call, a monthly dinner, a visit to someone housebound, a standing seat at a parish group. Make it small enough to survive a bad week. Tell someone so they can ask how it is going.',
  saint='Aelred of Rievaulx: a twelfth-century abbot who wrote Spiritual Friendship, arguing that friendship is a school of love for God.',
  misc=[
    '“If I am lonely, something is wrong with me.”',
    '“Church friendships are not real friendships.”',
    '“Saints in heaven are too distant to be friends.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:intercession-of-saints', 'room:desales-devout-life'],
  mem='“No longer do I call you servants... but I have called you friends.” (John 15:15)',
  assess='Ask each person to explain in three sentences how the Church answers loneliness and name the one recurring act of presence they will take up.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask everyone to stand if they have eaten a meal in the last week with no phone in sight, and with another person. Count. Say, “That is a small measure. Let’s talk about why it matters.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Genesis 2 says: ‘It is not good that the man should be alone.’ The first not-good in the Bible is loneliness. The Catechism says we are made in the image of a God who is a communion of persons. So loneliness is not just a feeling. It is a sign that we were made for something.”',
      'Say: “The creed says, ‘I believe in the communion of saints.’ That does not only mean saints in heaven. It means everyone baptised, living or dead, is one family. We share prayer and sacrifice. When you pray, you are in a crowd. When you are suffering, someone who loves you is offering for you. The saints are not far. They are the older siblings in the family.”',
      'Say: “Friendship is not a luxury. The Catechism, 2347, says chastity shows itself in friendship, and that friendship is a great good for everyone. Friendship is charity at work. And there is a surprise at 1658. It says single people, widowed people, people living alone, are especially close to the heart of Jesus, and belong to the domestic church. You are not outside the family. You are in it.”',
      'Say: “Belonging has to be built. It comes from showing up, on a regular schedule, in small ways. A weekly call is better than a rare grand gesture. A seat you always take is better than a talk about community. What small, repeatable act of presence could you begin?”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Where did you feel most known in your life? What made it possible?',
      'Who in your life is waiting for you to make the first move?',
      'What small act of presence would you actually keep?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Pass out cards. Write three names: one you love, one you have drifted from, one you barely know.',
      'For each, write one specific act (call, dinner, invitation).',
      'Choose the first and send the message now.',
    ],
    close='Pray the grace before meals together. Say, “Eat with someone this week, and look at them.”'),
  tryit=['Write three names: love, drifted, barely known.', 'Choose one small act for each.', 'Send the first message now.'],
  saintStory='Aelred was born in Northumbria, England, around 1110. As a young man he served at the court of the king of Scotland and was destined for a career there. He was well liked, with a gift for friendship, and later wrote of the heartbreak of leaving his friends. Around 1134 he visited the new Cistercian abbey at Rievaulx in Yorkshire, and felt called to stay.\nHe became a monk, and in 1147, abbot, leading about 600 monks and lay brothers. He was known for his kindness and for being easy to approach. In Spiritual Friendship, a dialogue between friends, he argued that friendship is a gift from God, that it is a step toward the love of God, and that it is a form of the love Jesus describes at the Last Supper. Famously he wrote, “God is friendship.”\nHe suffered from kidney stones and arthritis, and for the last years was often in pain. He died in 1167, surrounded by his monks. His book is still read as one of the great Christian reflections on friendship.',
),
])
